\Cohstack_{X/B}$", "MATH_0007": "$B$", "MATH_0008": "$\\Cohstack_{X/B}$", "MATH_0009": "$U \\to \\Cohstack_{X/B}$", "MATH_0010": "$U$", "MATH_0011": "$\\Cohstack^{P'}_{X/B}$", "MATH_0012": "$U$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{lemma-coherent-qs-lfp}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{stacks-morphisms-lemma-locally-finite-type-locally-noetherian}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 2, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:0da042cf90f4e515df4f3942ebe48dd7777dc197dab0b87d45494d1eff115bcb", "source_math_skeleton_hash": "sha256:2717a63040bf56216977dc3978c87925cb43896ea2c59f071086b5f0d560c821", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:4f8a6e9b14d69d7124e09d80a3ffb62e9afb74961306938e04384e105a2b4be8", "source_text": "This is immediately clear if <MATH_0001> is finite, because the functions\n<MATH_0002> are locally constant. If <MATH_0003> is infinite, then\nwe write\n<MATHSEG_0001> finite<MATHSEG_0002>\nand we denote <MATH_0004>. Then we have\n<MATHSEG_0003> finite<MATHSEG_0004>\nTherefore, <MATH_0005> is always an algebraic stack and the morphism\n<MATH_0006> is always a flat closed immersion,\nbut it may no longer be an open substack. (We leave it to the reader to\nmake examples). However, if <MATH_0007> is locally Noetherian, then so\nis <MATH_0008> by Lemma <REF_0001> and\nMorphisms of Stacks, Lemma\n<REF_0002>.\nHence if <MATH_0009> is a smooth surjective morphism\nwhere <MATH_0010> is a locally Noetherian scheme, then the inverse images of\nthe open and closed substacks <MATH_0011>\nhave an open intersection in <MATH_0012> (because connected components of\nlocally Noetherian topological spaces are open).\nThus the result in this case.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:3965726addf45325df590fc77b14e02d179fb40884cbe8d4cb7e3dbd2f094f17", "unit_id": "tag:0DND:proof"}
